<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aden</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aden is a port city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, built in the crater of an extinct volcano near the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. For centuries a hub of trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Yemeni dynasties such as the Zurayids and Rasulids, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1839 and became a major coaling station and colony. It was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> from 1967 to 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aden.jpg" alt="Aden" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmedxalkatheri · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MallaDistrict.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…long-distance trade: frankincense and myrrh from the south and east, and the transit of Indian and African goods through ports such as Muza and <b>Aden</b>, described in the first-century Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, which names Charibael (Karib'il) as king of the Homerites and Sabaeans, ruling from Saphar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Yemen was formed on 22 May 1990 by the union of the Yemen Arab Republic in the north, with its capital at Sanaa, and the People's Democratic Republic of Yemen, or South Yemen, with its capital at <b>Aden</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 2015 the kingdom mobilised 150,000 troops and 100 fighter jets against the Houthis in Yemen, taking <b>Aden</b> by early 2016 while Houthi attacks crossed the border; 2,500 Saudi troops were still engaged in 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain had held <b>Aden</b> since 1839 and built it into a major port and military base, while the sultanates, emirates and sheikhdoms of its hinterland formed the Aden Protectorate under British treaties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>To secure the Aden Settlement and keep the Ottoman Empire away from it, Britain signed treaties with the tribal rulers around <b>Aden</b> from the 1870s, beginning with the Sultanate of Lahej and its neighbours, and later with the Hadrami states.</blockquote><a href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1839 forces of the British East India Company under Captain Stafford Haines captured <b>Aden</b> from the Sultanate of Lahej after a dispute over a plundered ship.</blockquote><a href="/state/aden-settlement">Aden Settlement</a></li></ul></section>
